# Step 7: Repository Layer & First API Endpoints (Pre-DTO Phase)

## Purpose of This Step

This step establishes the **core backend architecture** by introducing:

* Repository pattern (interfaces + concrete classes)
* Dependency Injection (DI)
* Clean controllers using repositories
* First working CRUD API endpoints
* Understanding why APIs return nested entities
* Entity design decisions that affect API behavior
* Clear rules for when migrations are required
* Conceptual justification for DTOs (implementation comes later)

At this stage:
* Authentication ❌
* DTO implementation ❌
* Repository + Controller architecture ✅

## 1. Repository Layer — Why It Exists

The Repository Layer sits between controllers and the database:
Controller → Repository → DbContext → Database
### Responsibilities

* Encapsulate all database operations
* Hide EF Core queries from controllers
* Centralize data access logic
* Improve testability and maintainability
* Prevent controllers from depending directly on `DbContext`

Controllers should **never** talk directly to `ApplicationDbContext`.

## 2. Interfaces vs Concrete Classes (Core Concept)

### Interfaces — the Contract

Interfaces define **what operations are allowed**, not how they are implemented.

Example:
public interface IUserRepository
{
    Task<IEnumerable<User>> GetAllUsersAsync();
    Task<User?> GetUserByIdAsync(int id);
    Task<User> AddUserAsync(User user);
    Task<User?> UpdateUserAsync(User user);
    Task<bool> DeleteUserAsync(int id);
}
Key points:

* No logic
* No EF Core code
* Only method signatures
* Forces consistency across implementations

This enables **loose coupling**.

### Concrete Classes — the Implementation

Concrete repositories **fulfill the contract** defined by interfaces.

Example:
public class UserRepository : IUserRepository
{
    private readonly ApplicationDbContext _context;

    public UserRepository(ApplicationDbContext context)
    {
        _context = context;
    }

    public async Task<IEnumerable<User>> GetAllUsersAsync()
        => await _context.Users.ToListAsync();
}
Key points:

* Implements the interface
* Contains EF Core logic
* Talks to the database
* Can be replaced later without touching controllers

## 3. Dependency Injection (DI) — How Everything Is Wired

### What is Dependency Injection?

Dependency Injection means:

> A class receives its dependencies instead of creating them itself.

Controllers depend on **interfaces**, not concrete implementations.

Example:
public class UsersController : ControllerBase
{
    private readonly IUserRepository _userRepository;

    public UsersController(IUserRepository userRepository)
    {
        _userRepository = userRepository;
    }
}

The controller does **not** know which class implements `IUserRepository`.

### Registering Repositories in Program.cs
builder.Services.AddScoped<IUserRepository, UserRepository>();
builder.Services.AddScoped<ITaskRepository, TaskRepository>();

This tells ASP.NET Core:

> “Whenever a controller asks for `IUserRepository`, provide `UserRepository`.”

### Why `AddScoped`?

* One instance per HTTP request
* Matches EF Core `DbContext` lifetime
* Prevents data conflicts
* Best practice for repositories
## 4. Controllers — Thin HTTP Layers

Controllers should:

* Handle HTTP requests
* Call repository methods
* Return HTTP responses
* Contain **no database logic**

Example:
[HttpGet]
public async Task<IActionResult> GetAll()
{
    var users = await _userRepository.GetAllUsersAsync();
    return Ok(users);
}


Controllers do **not**:

* Write SQL
* Use `DbContext`
* Contain business rules

## 5. Repository Layer Does NOT Control API Response Shape

Important clarification:

> Repositories return **Entities**, not API responses.

If a controller returns an entity directly:

return Ok(user);
Then the API response will include:

* All entity properties
* All navigation properties
* Nested related data

This behavior is expected.

## 6. Why API Endpoints Return Nested Entities

Entities contain navigation properties:
public ICollection<TaskItem> AssignedTasks { get; set; }
public ICollection<TaskStatusHistory> StatusHistories { get; set; }
When returned from a controller:

* ASP.NET Core serializes everything
* Swagger expands relationships
* Nested JSON appears automatically

Key insight:
> **Entity = database model, not API contract**

This is the reason DTOs will be introduced later.
## 7. Required vs Optional Properties (Entity Design)

### Primary Key
public int Id { get; set; }

* Not required
* Generated by database
* Never sent by clients

### Core Business Fields
public string FullName { get; set; }
public string Email { get; set; }

* Conceptually required
* Validation will be enforced later via DTOs

### System-Managed Fields
public string PasswordHash { get; set; }
public string Role { get; set; }
public DateTime CreatedAt { get; set; }

* Set internally
* Not provided by clients
* Should not be exposed in API responses

### Navigation Properties
public ICollection<TaskItem> AssignedTasks { get; set; }

Rules:
* Never required
* Never sent by client
* Managed by EF Core
* Initialize to empty collections

## 8. When Is a Migration Required?

### ❌ Migration NOT required when:

* Adding or removing `required`
* Adding validation attributes
* Changing serialization behavior
* Controller or repository logic changes

### ✅ Migration REQUIRED when:

* Adding/removing entity properties
* Changing property data types
* Adding foreign keys
* Changing relationships
* Altering database schema

Rule:

> If the SQL table structure changes → run a migration.

## 9. Why This Step Naturally Leads to DTOs

At the end of this step:

* Repositories work correctly
* Controllers work correctly
* APIs return too much data
* Swagger schemas look messy

This is **not a mistake** — it’s the natural point where DTOs become necessary.

DTOs will:

* Control request & response payloads
* Hide navigation properties
* Prevent over-posting
* Decouple API contracts from database models

